Marking -> finding and marking live objects (start from roots viz., thread stacks, classloaders, static classes) 

Sweep -> Going over objects in your heap and adding unmarked objects to a free list (yes your free list *will* contain discontiguous objects)

Compaction ->  Moving all live objects to a contiguous range either via copying or via in place moves


------------------

Concurrent GCs do their thing (all or some of the above) while the mutator is working (mutator is what changes the heap by assigning object
references)

Parallel GCs do their thing using multiple threads

------------------

Generally compaction is what is done via stop-the-world locks. In generational GCs the copy operation is also stop the world.

More details to come involving read barriers ...

------------------

Only compacting heaps can do bump pointer style allocations. ( in most cases it turns out to be the generational ones that use compaction 
or rather copying)

Non generational non compacting ones still have to do free list walks

